        "name": "DesignFileFlow",\
        "states": [\
          "uploaded",\
          "processing",\
          "ready",\
          "approved",\
          "error"\
        ],\
        "initial": "uploaded",\
        "transitions": [\
          \{\
            "from": "uploaded",\
            "to": "processing",\
            "action": "Process",\
            "role": "any"\
          \},\
          \{\
            "from": "processing",\
            "to": "ready",\
            "action": "Complete",\
            "role": "any"\
          \},\
          \{\
            "from": "processing",\
            "to": "error",\
            "action": "Fail",\
            "role": "any"\
          \},\
          \{\
            "from": "error",\
            "to": "processing",\
            "action": "Retry Conversion",\
            "role": "design_user"\
          \},\
          \{\
            "from": "ready",\
            "to": "approved",\
            "action": "Approve Release",\
            "role": "design_admin",\
            "_comment": "CAD-41 \'97 this was `\\"role\\": \\"any\\"`. Releasing a design is the gate into manufacturing: entering `approved` runs designreview/on_design_file_approved, and the DesignVersion \uc0\u8594  DesignBOM \u8594  Item/Routing/BOM chain refuses to run until the parent file is approved. So `any` meant a read-only viewer \'97 typically the external or vendor persona \'97 could release a design for manufacture and start the product. `design_admin` is the role that holds `delete` on DesignFile; releasing is at least that consequential. `design_user` may still reopen, so an engineer is not locked out of their own work."\
          \},\
          \{\
            "from": "approved",\
            "to": "ready",\
            "action": "Reopen for Design",\
            "role": "design_user",\
            "_comment": "Reopening un-releases a file; it does not start manufacture, so it stays with the engineers rather than with `any`."\
          \}\
        ]\
      \}\
    \},\
